{"type":"message","id":"8d1f7bf4","parentId":"6499f31e","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:29.841Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_Pab2RC5e7AsFQ1nZdC69VHmS","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -RinE 'Peirce|tokenized stocks|innovation exemption|tokenized stock' /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds /home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher | head -80","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":60}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":88401,"output":681,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":89082,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779437957022,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiFOA6t3h8N1eBPBeHH7hISnK9j0b"}}
{"type":"message","id":"14350d4c","parentId":"8d1f7bf4","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:29.950Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_Pab2RC5e7AsFQ1nZdC69VHmS","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:83:    <title>SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce Clarifies Limits on Tokenized Stocks</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:84:    <link>https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:85:    <description>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner Hester Peirce recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed \"innovation exemption\" for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, P…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:87:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner <strong>Hester Peirce</strong> recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed <strong>\"innovation exemption\"</strong> for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, Peirce addressed what she described as an overinterpretation of the proposal, emphasizing that the exemption is intended to facilitate the digital representation of <strong>existing equity securities</strong> rather than the creation of entirely new financial instruments. This clarification aims to set realistic expectations for the integration of traditional finance and blockchain technology.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:88:<h2>Scope of the Innovation Exemption</h2>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:89:<p>According to the Commissioner, the primary objective of the exemption is to allow for the <strong>tokenization of stocks already tradable</strong> in the secondary market. By utilizing a national market system for digital representations, the SEC seeks to streamline the movement of traditional assets across distributed ledger technology (DLT) without bypassing established regulatory frameworks. Peirce explicitly noted that the scope does not extend to:</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:95:<p>The SEC’s cautious approach highlights the ongoing tension between rapid technological advancement in the <strong>DeFi</strong> space and the protection of retail investors within the <strong>national market system</strong>. By limiting the \"innovation exemption\" to existing securities, the regulator ensures that tokenized versions of stocks like Apple or Tesla remain subject to the same disclosure and transparency requirements as their traditional counterparts. This stance signals that while the <strong>blockchain</strong> may serve as the underlying infrastructure, the underlying regulatory principles of the U.S. financial markets remain unchanged.</p>]]></content:encoded>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:97:    <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:209:    <title>Hester Peirce to Join Regent University Law School in Late 2026</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:210:    <link>https://cryptofox.news/news/hester-peirce-to-join-regent-university-law-school-in-late-2026</link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:211:    <description>Hester Peirce, a prominent Commissioner at the United States Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), is set to transition into academia. According to reports from Fox Business journalist Eleanor Terrett and an official announcement from Regent University Law School in Virgin…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:213:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Hester Peirce, a prominent Commissioner at the <strong>United States Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)</strong>, is set to transition into academia. According to reports from Fox Business journalist Eleanor Terrett and an official announcement from <strong>Regent University Law School</strong> in Virginia, Peirce will join the faculty as an associate professor in <strong>November 2026</strong>. This move signals the potential conclusion of her influential tenure at the regulatory agency, where she has been a vocal advocate for the digital asset sector.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:215:<p>At Regent University, Peirce is expected to leverage her extensive experience in federal oversight to lead specialized courses. Her curriculum will focus on critical areas of modern finance, including <strong>securities regulation, financial markets, public policy</strong>, and the burgeoning field of <strong>digital assets</strong>. <em>The appointment highlights a growing trend of high-level regulators bringing practical expertise to legal education to prepare the next generation of attorneys for complex market dynamics.</em></p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:216:<p>Peirce’s departure timeline aligns with the scheduled conclusion of her term, marking the end of an era characterized by her frequent dissents against the SEC's \"regulation by enforcement\" approach toward the <strong>blockchain industry</strong>. Throughout her service, she has consistently called for a clearer legal framework for <strong>Bitcoin (BTC), Ethereum (ETH)</strong>, and other cryptocurrencies.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:218:<p>Known widely by the moniker <strong>\"Crypto Mom,\"</strong> Peirce earned a reputation for championing innovation and demanding transparency within the commission. Her efforts have been centered on several key initiatives:</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:220:<blockquote>\"Peirce will teach courses in securities regulation, financial markets, digital assets, and public policy\", the university's statement confirmed, underlining her transition from policy-making to academic instruction.</blockquote>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:221:<p>The transition of Commissioner Peirce is expected to be a significant milestone for the <strong>crypto-regulatory landscape</strong> in the United States. As she prepares to depart in 2026, the industry remains attentive to who might succeed her and whether her philosophy of <strong>regulatory clarity</strong> and market-friendly oversight will persist within the commission. Her move to Regent University ensures that her influence will continue to shape the legal discourse surrounding <strong>decentralized finance (DeFi)</strong> and traditional capital markets for years to come.</p>]]></content:encoded>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:223:    <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cryptofox.news/news/hester-peirce-to-join-regent-university-law-school-in-late-2026</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:339:    <title>SEC to Unveil Innovation Exemption for Tokenized Stocks This Week</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:341:    <description>The U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is reportedly preparing to introduce a landmark “innovation exemption” framework as early as this week. According to Bloomberg sources, this new regulatory regime aims to facilitate the trading of tokenized versions of shares i…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:343:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>The U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is reportedly preparing to introduce a landmark <strong>“innovation exemption”</strong> framework as early as this week. According to Bloomberg sources, this new regulatory regime aims to facilitate the trading of <strong>tokenized versions of shares</strong> in publicly listed companies. The initiative, spearheaded under the leadership of SEC Chair <strong>Paul Atkins</strong>, represents a significant shift toward integrating blockchain technology into traditional equity markets, potentially allowing digital versions of securities to trade on decentralized platforms.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:348:<p>While SEC Commissioner <strong>Hester Peirce</strong>—often referred to as \"Crypto Mom\"—has been a key advocate for this \"sandbox-style\" approach, the proposal has faced internal friction and external pushback from major financial institutions. Giants such as <strong>Citadel Securities</strong> and the Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association (<strong>SIFMA</strong>) have voiced concerns regarding market fragmentation. These entities argue that the move could weaken critical <strong>Know Your Customer (KYC)</strong> and anti-money laundering (AML) protocols that have long anchored U.S. capital markets.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:351:<p>The rollout of the innovation exemption is viewed as a pilot test to determine if the <strong>T+0 atomic settlement</strong> capabilities of blockchain can improve efficiency without sacrificing investor protections. This follows the recent approvals for <strong>Nasdaq</strong> and the <strong>NYSE</strong> to trade tokenized equities within restricted pilot programs earlier in 2026. By moving these activities to decentralized protocols, the SEC aims to gather data on the viability of a permanent regulatory framework for the <strong>on-chain trading</strong> of traditional securities.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:62:    <title>SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce Clarifies Limits on Tokenized Stocks</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:63:    <link>https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:64:    <description>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner Hester Peirce recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed \"innovation exemption\" for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, P…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:66:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner <strong>Hester Peirce</strong> recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed <strong>\"innovation exemption\"</strong> for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, Peirce addressed what she described as an overinterpretation of the proposal, emphasizing that the exemption is intended to facilitate the digital representation of <strong>existing equity securities</strong> rather than the creation of entirely new financial instruments. This clarification aims to set realistic expectations for the integration of traditional finance and blockchain technology.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:67:<h2>Scope of the Innovation Exemption</h2>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:68:<p>According to the Commissioner, the primary objective of the exemption is to allow for the <strong>tokenization of stocks already tradable</strong> in the secondary market. By utilizing a national market system for digital representations, the SEC seeks to streamline the movement of traditional assets across distributed ledger technology (DLT) without bypassing established regulatory frameworks. Peirce explicitly noted that the scope does not extend to:</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:74:<p>The SEC’s cautious approach highlights the ongoing tension between rapid technological advancement in the <strong>DeFi</strong> space and the protection of retail investors within the <strong>national market system</strong>. By limiting the \"innovation exemption\" to existing securities, the regulator ensures that tokenized versions of stocks like Apple or Tesla remain subject to the same disclosure and transparency requirements as their traditional counterparts. This stance signals that while the <strong>blockchain</strong> may serve as the underlying infrastructure, the underlying regulatory principles of the U.S. financial markets remain unchanged.</p>]]></content:encoded>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:76:    <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:20:      <title>Tokenized stocks risk liquidity and revenue fragmentation: Research</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:25:      <description><![CDATA[<p style=\"float:right; margin:0 0 10px 15px; width:240px;\"><img src=\"https://images.cointelegraph.com/images/528_aHR0cHM6Ly9zMy1pbWFnZXMuY3RtZWRpYS5pby9tZWRpYS9hcnRpY2xlLWNvdmVycy9kaWdpdGFsLWJvbmRzLW9uLWJsb2NrY2hhaW4tMi5qcGc=.jpg\" alt=\"Tokenized stocks risk liquidity and revenue fragmentation: Research\" class=\"type:primaryImage\"></p><p>TradFi views the breakup of its previously consolidated, centralized liquidity as a “serious structural threat,” said Tiger Research director Ryan Yoon.\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:45:      <title>SEC's Peirce tempers expectations over tokenized stocks exemption</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:48:      <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cointelegraph.com/news/secs-peirce-tempers-expectations-over-innovation-exemption-for-tokenized-stocks</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:49:      <link><![CDATA[https://cointelegraph.com/news/secs-peirce-tempers-expectations-over-innovation-exemption-for-tokenized-stocks?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound]]></link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:50:      <description><![CDATA[<p style=\"float:right; margin:0 0 10px 15px; width:240px;\"><img src=\"https://images.cointelegraph.com/images/528_aHR0cHM6Ly9zMy1pbWFnZXMuY3RtZWRpYS5pby9tZWRpYS9hcnRpY2xlLWNvdmVycy9oZXN0ZXItcGVpcmNlLXNlYy5qcGc=.jpg\" alt=\"SEC's Peirce tempers expectations over tokenized stocks exemption\" class=\"type:primaryImage\"></p><p>An executive at tokenization platforms Superstate said the stricter approach suggested by Hester Peirce would enable DeFi to expand without compromising rules in traditional capital markets.</p>]]></description>\n/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher/cointelegraph_new.xml:345:      <title>Ondo brings proxy voting to tokenized stocks and ETFs with Broadridge</title>\n/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher/cointelegraph_new.xml:350:      <description><![CDATA[<p style=\"float:right; margin:0 0 10px 15px; width:240px;\"><img src=\"https://images.cointelegraph.com/images/528_aHR0cHM6Ly9wYXlsb2FkLmx1bS10cmkub3JnL2FwaS9hcnRpY2xlLWNvdmVycy9maWxlLzEuanBnP3ByZWZpeD1tZWRpYSUyRmFydGljbGUtY292ZXJz.jpg\" alt=\"Ondo brings proxy voting to tokenized stocks and ETFs with Broadridge\" class=\"type:primaryImage\"></p><p>Tokenized stock holders can now participate in corporate governance, closing a long-standing gap between onchain securities and their traditional counterparts.</p>]]></description>"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":56,"aggregated":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:83:    <title>SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce Clarifies Limits on Tokenized Stocks</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:84:    <link>https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:85:    <description>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner Hester Peirce recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed \"innovation exemption\" for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, P…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:87:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner <strong>Hester Peirce</strong> recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed <strong>\"innovation exemption\"</strong> for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, Peirce addressed what she described as an overinterpretation of the proposal, emphasizing that the exemption is intended to facilitate the digital representation of <strong>existing equity securities</strong> rather than the creation of entirely new financial instruments. This clarification aims to set realistic expectations for the integration of traditional finance and blockchain technology.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:88:<h2>Scope of the Innovation Exemption</h2>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:89:<p>According to the Commissioner, the primary objective of the exemption is to allow for the <strong>tokenization of stocks already tradable</strong> in the secondary market. By utilizing a national market system for digital representations, the SEC seeks to streamline the movement of traditional assets across distributed ledger technology (DLT) without bypassing established regulatory frameworks. Peirce explicitly noted that the scope does not extend to:</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:95:<p>The SEC’s cautious approach highlights the ongoing tension between rapid technological advancement in the <strong>DeFi</strong> space and the protection of retail investors within the <strong>national market system</strong>. By limiting the \"innovation exemption\" to existing securities, the regulator ensures that tokenized versions of stocks like Apple or Tesla remain subject to the same disclosure and transparency requirements as their traditional counterparts. This stance signals that while the <strong>blockchain</strong> may serve as the underlying infrastructure, the underlying regulatory principles of the U.S. financial markets remain unchanged.</p>]]></content:encoded>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:97:    <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:209:    <title>Hester Peirce to Join Regent University Law School in Late 2026</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:210:    <link>https://cryptofox.news/news/hester-peirce-to-join-regent-university-law-school-in-late-2026</link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:211:    <description>Hester Peirce, a prominent Commissioner at the United States Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), is set to transition into academia. According to reports from Fox Business journalist Eleanor Terrett and an official announcement from Regent University Law School in Virgin…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:213:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Hester Peirce, a prominent Commissioner at the <strong>United States Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)</strong>, is set to transition into academia. According to reports from Fox Business journalist Eleanor Terrett and an official announcement from <strong>Regent University Law School</strong> in Virginia, Peirce will join the faculty as an associate professor in <strong>November 2026</strong>. This move signals the potential conclusion of her influential tenure at the regulatory agency, where she has been a vocal advocate for the digital asset sector.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:215:<p>At Regent University, Peirce is expected to leverage her extensive experience in federal oversight to lead specialized courses. Her curriculum will focus on critical areas of modern finance, including <strong>securities regulation, financial markets, public policy</strong>, and the burgeoning field of <strong>digital assets</strong>. <em>The appointment highlights a growing trend of high-level regulators bringing practical expertise to legal education to prepare the next generation of attorneys for complex market dynamics.</em></p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:216:<p>Peirce’s departure timeline aligns with the scheduled conclusion of her term, marking the end of an era characterized by her frequent dissents against the SEC's \"regulation by enforcement\" approach toward the <strong>blockchain industry</strong>. Throughout her service, she has consistently called for a clearer legal framework for <strong>Bitcoin (BTC), Ethereum (ETH)</strong>, and other cryptocurrencies.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:218:<p>Known widely by the moniker <strong>\"Crypto Mom,\"</strong> Peirce earned a reputation for championing innovation and demanding transparency within the commission. Her efforts have been centered on several key initiatives:</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:220:<blockquote>\"Peirce will teach courses in securities regulation, financial markets, digital assets, and public policy\", the university's statement confirmed, underlining her transition from policy-making to academic instruction.</blockquote>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:221:<p>The transition of Commissioner Peirce is expected to be a significant milestone for the <strong>crypto-regulatory landscape</strong> in the United States. As she prepares to depart in 2026, the industry remains attentive to who might succeed her and whether her philosophy of <strong>regulatory clarity</strong> and market-friendly oversight will persist within the commission. Her move to Regent University ensures that her influence will continue to shape the legal discourse surrounding <strong>decentralized finance (DeFi)</strong> and traditional capital markets for years to come.</p>]]></content:encoded>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:223:    <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cryptofox.news/news/hester-peirce-to-join-regent-university-law-school-in-late-2026</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:339:    <title>SEC to Unveil Innovation Exemption for Tokenized Stocks This Week</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:341:    <description>The U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is reportedly preparing to introduce a landmark “innovation exemption” framework as early as this week. According to Bloomberg sources, this new regulatory regime aims to facilitate the trading of tokenized versions of shares i…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:343:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>The U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is reportedly preparing to introduce a landmark <strong>“innovation exemption”</strong> framework as early as this week. According to Bloomberg sources, this new regulatory regime aims to facilitate the trading of <strong>tokenized versions of shares</strong> in publicly listed companies. The initiative, spearheaded under the leadership of SEC Chair <strong>Paul Atkins</strong>, represents a significant shift toward integrating blockchain technology into traditional equity markets, potentially allowing digital versions of securities to trade on decentralized platforms.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:348:<p>While SEC Commissioner <strong>Hester Peirce</strong>—often referred to as \"Crypto Mom\"—has been a key advocate for this \"sandbox-style\" approach, the proposal has faced internal friction and external pushback from major financial institutions. Giants such as <strong>Citadel Securities</strong> and the Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association (<strong>SIFMA</strong>) have voiced concerns regarding market fragmentation. These entities argue that the move could weaken critical <strong>Know Your Customer (KYC)</strong> and anti-money laundering (AML) protocols that have long anchored U.S. capital markets.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_regulation.xml:351:<p>The rollout of the innovation exemption is viewed as a pilot test to determine if the <strong>T+0 atomic settlement</strong> capabilities of blockchain can improve efficiency without sacrificing investor protections. This follows the recent approvals for <strong>Nasdaq</strong> and the <strong>NYSE</strong> to trade tokenized equities within restricted pilot programs earlier in 2026. By moving these activities to decentralized protocols, the SEC aims to gather data on the viability of a permanent regulatory framework for the <strong>on-chain trading</strong> of traditional securities.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:62:    <title>SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce Clarifies Limits on Tokenized Stocks</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:63:    <link>https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:64:    <description>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner Hester Peirce recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed \"innovation exemption\" for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, P…</description>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:66:    <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Commissioner <strong>Hester Peirce</strong> recently clarified the regulatory boundaries regarding the proposed <strong>\"innovation exemption\"</strong> for the on-chain trading of tokenized stocks. In a statement released on the social platform X on May 21, 2026, Peirce addressed what she described as an overinterpretation of the proposal, emphasizing that the exemption is intended to facilitate the digital representation of <strong>existing equity securities</strong> rather than the creation of entirely new financial instruments. This clarification aims to set realistic expectations for the integration of traditional finance and blockchain technology.</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:67:<h2>Scope of the Innovation Exemption</h2>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:68:<p>According to the Commissioner, the primary objective of the exemption is to allow for the <strong>tokenization of stocks already tradable</strong> in the secondary market. By utilizing a national market system for digital representations, the SEC seeks to streamline the movement of traditional assets across distributed ledger technology (DLT) without bypassing established regulatory frameworks. Peirce explicitly noted that the scope does not extend to:</p>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:74:<p>The SEC’s cautious approach highlights the ongoing tension between rapid technological advancement in the <strong>DeFi</strong> space and the protection of retail investors within the <strong>national market system</strong>. By limiting the \"innovation exemption\" to existing securities, the regulator ensures that tokenized versions of stocks like Apple or Tesla remain subject to the same disclosure and transparency requirements as their traditional counterparts. This stance signals that while the <strong>blockchain</strong> may serve as the underlying infrastructure, the underlying regulatory principles of the U.S. financial markets remain unchanged.</p>]]></content:encoded>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cryptofox_markets.xml:76:    <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cryptofox.news/news/sec-commissioner-hester-peirce-clarifies-limits-on-tokenized-stocks</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:20:      <title>Tokenized stocks risk liquidity and revenue fragmentation: Research</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:25:      <description><![CDATA[<p style=\"float:right; margin:0 0 10px 15px; width:240px;\"><img src=\"https://images.cointelegraph.com/images/528_aHR0cHM6Ly9zMy1pbWFnZXMuY3RtZWRpYS5pby9tZWRpYS9hcnRpY2xlLWNvdmVycy9kaWdpdGFsLWJvbmRzLW9uLWJsb2NrY2hhaW4tMi5qcGc=.jpg\" alt=\"Tokenized stocks risk liquidity and revenue fragmentation: Research\" class=\"type:primaryImage\"></p><p>TradFi views the breakup of its previously consolidated, centralized liquidity as a “serious structural threat,” said Tiger Research director Ryan Yoon.\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:45:      <title>SEC's Peirce tempers expectations over tokenized stocks exemption</title>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:48:      <guid isPermaLink=\"true\">https://cointelegraph.com/news/secs-peirce-tempers-expectations-over-innovation-exemption-for-tokenized-stocks</guid>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:49:      <link><![CDATA[https://cointelegraph.com/news/secs-peirce-tempers-expectations-over-innovation-exemption-for-tokenized-stocks?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound]]></link>\n/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/feeds/cointelegraph.xml:50:      <description><![CDATA[<p style=\"float:right; margin:0 0 10px 15px; width:240px;\"><img src=\"https://images.cointelegraph.com/images/528_aHR0cHM6Ly9zMy1pbWFnZXMuY3RtZWRpYS5pby9tZWRpYS9hcnRpY2xlLWNvdmVycy9oZXN0ZXItcGVpcmNlLXNlYy5qcGc=.jpg\" alt=\"SEC's Peirce tempers expectations over tokenized stocks exemption\" class=\"type:primaryImage\"></p><p>An executive at tokenization platforms Superstate said the stricter approach suggested by Hester Peirce would enable DeFi to expand without compromising rules in traditional capital markets.</p>]]></description>\n/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher/cointelegraph_new.xml:345:      <title>Ondo brings proxy voting to tokenized stocks and ETFs with Broadridge</title>\n/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher/cointelegraph_new.xml:350:      <description><![CDATA[<p style=\"float:right; margin:0 0 10px 15px; width:240px;\"><img src=\"https://images.cointelegraph.com/images/528_aHR0cHM6Ly9wYXlsb2FkLmx1bS10cmkub3JnL2FwaS9hcnRpY2xlLWNvdmVycy9maWxlLzEuanBnP3ByZWZpeD1tZWRpYSUyRmFydGljbGUtY292ZXJz.jpg\" alt=\"Ondo brings proxy voting to tokenized stocks and ETFs with Broadridge\" class=\"type:primaryImage\"></p><p>Tokenized stock holders can now participate in corporate governance, closing a long-standing gap between onchain securities and their traditional counterparts.</p>]]></description>","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779437969950}}
{"type":"message","id":"d3428535","parentId":"14350d4c","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:41.000Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_nsYXoVA4yRDIwUhSUtImrz5s","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"for u in \\\n 'https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law' \\\n 'https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/' \\\n 'https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve'; do echo 'URL' $u; curl -o /dev/null -s -w '%{http_code} %{url_effective}\\n' -L \"$u\"; done","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":20000,"timeout":60}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ix4roqyksRKwt6wBXbvI3HsK","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"mkdir -p /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles\ntrafilatura -u 'https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law' --output-format markdown > /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/decrypt_arma.md || true\nwc -c /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/decrypt_arma.md\nhead -c 5000 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/decrypt_arma.md","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":30000,"timeout":120}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_alr1lh3gRuGPrUrZ03yKrygw","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"mkdir -p /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles\ntrafilatura -u 'https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/' --output-format markdown > /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptoslate_arma.md || true\nwc -c /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptoslate_arma.md\nhead -c 5000 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptoslate_arma.md","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":30000,"timeout":120}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7ewiJbnT4g8HhVyu8M5LMPLm","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"mkdir -p /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles\ntrafilatura -u 'https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve' --output-format markdown > /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptofox_arma.md || true\nwc -c /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptofox_arma.md\nhead -c 5000 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptofox_arma.md","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":30000,"timeout":120}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":92306,"output":828,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":93134,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779437969958,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiFONrPYPd6QDsbdRycQDdRAGF8ig"}}
{"type":"message","id":"247ddf64","parentId":"d3428535","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:47.864Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_nsYXoVA4yRDIwUhSUtImrz5s","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"URL https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n200 https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\nURL https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\n200 https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\nURL https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\n200 https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":6055,"aggregated":"URL https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n200 https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\nURL https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\n200 https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\nURL https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\n200 https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779437987864}}
{"type":"message","id":"d54090b2","parentId":"247ddf64","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:47.864Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ix4roqyksRKwt6wBXbvI3HsK","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3868 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/decrypt_arma.md\n#### In brief\n\n- Reps. Nick Begich (R-AK) and Jared Golden (D-ME) introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act.\n- The bill seeks formally codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law, which would be managed for at least 20 years.\n- Aimed at cementing a core Trump administration promise, the initiative has faced ongoing hurdles.\n\nRep. Nick Begich (R-AK) introduced legislation on Thursday aimed at enshrining a strategic reserve for Bitcoin in federal law, seeking to cement one of President Donald Trump’s core campaign promises for digital asset holders ahead of the high-stakes U.S. midterm elections.\n\nThe American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a reserve of the digital asset for a minimum of 20 years, while establishing a stockpile for other cryptocurrencies held by the government.\n\nThe legislation also mandates the consolidation of what is believed to be billions of dollars in digital assets held across federal agencies—attained through actions like forfeitures and penalties—while providing transparency through a proof-of-reserve report.\n\nAs of last month, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin that Trump created via executive order is still a work in progress. At a conference in Las Vegas, Patrick Witt, executive director of the President’s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, said the current administration has spent months “figuring out” the legal interpretations associated with the initiative.\n\nAmerica’s reserves balance sheet is a critical component of our nation’s insurance policy, bolstering our currency and providing assurance during times of uncertainty. Over time, the prevailing sentiment as to what constitutes a durable store of value can shift, and as such it is… pic.twitter.com/7XfBwAWFGi\n\n— Congressman Nick Begich (@RepNickBegich) May 21, 2026\n\n\nJoining Begich as the bill’s primary Democratic co-lead is Rep. Jared Golden (D-ME). The ARMA Act meanwhile counts 17 original co-sponsors, including Rep. Mike Collins (R-GA), among the first lawmakers to disclose an investment in a meme coin on Capitol Hill.\n\n“Administrations have auctioned [crypto] off or held it in reserve, according to the whims of the executive branch,” Golden said in a statement, noting that a stockpile created under the ARMA Act would enjoy the “weight of law.”\n\nAlthough a former administration official had said that the White House was eager to purchase as much Bitcoin as possible in a “budget-neutral way,” Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent later dented enthusiasm by ruling out any agency purchases.\n\nThe bill is aimed at providing the flexibility needed to broaden America’s portfolio of reserve assets that effectively serve as insurance policy, while also safeguarding crypto from “the whims of Congress or future administrations,” Begich said in an X post on Thursday.\n\nEstablished in May 2025 with a stroke of the president’s pen, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin was swiftly skewered by critics, including Rep. Maxine Waters (D-CA), the ranking member of the House Financial Services Committee, who warned of benefits for Trump insiders.\n\n“Typically, when the government holds strategic reserves, it is for an essential input that powers the U.S. economy and day-to-day life for American families,” she said. “Crypto, however, does not fall into these categories, because it has no inherent value.”\n\nAlthough the much-hyped stockpile’s creation represented a symbolic win for the digital assets industry, the government has yet to announce any allocations. Last year, Sen. Cynthia Lummis (R-WY) re-introduced the BITCOIN Act, a bill that directs the Treasury to purchase 1 million Bitcoin over five years in a way that seeks to avoid taxing citizens.\n\n*Decrypt* has reached out to Begich and Golden for comment."}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":1483,"aggregated":"3868 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/decrypt_arma.md\n#### In brief\n\n- Reps. Nick Begich (R-AK) and Jared Golden (D-ME) introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act.\n- The bill seeks formally codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law, which would be managed for at least 20 years.\n- Aimed at cementing a core Trump administration promise, the initiative has faced ongoing hurdles.\n\nRep. Nick Begich (R-AK) introduced legislation on Thursday aimed at enshrining a strategic reserve for Bitcoin in federal law, seeking to cement one of President Donald Trump’s core campaign promises for digital asset holders ahead of the high-stakes U.S. midterm elections.\n\nThe American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a reserve of the digital asset for a minimum of 20 years, while establishing a stockpile for other cryptocurrencies held by the government.\n\nThe legislation also mandates the consolidation of what is believed to be billions of dollars in digital assets held across federal agencies—attained through actions like forfeitures and penalties—while providing transparency through a proof-of-reserve report.\n\nAs of last month, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin that Trump created via executive order is still a work in progress. At a conference in Las Vegas, Patrick Witt, executive director of the President’s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, said the current administration has spent months “figuring out” the legal interpretations associated with the initiative.\n\nAmerica’s reserves balance sheet is a critical component of our nation’s insurance policy, bolstering our currency and providing assurance during times of uncertainty. Over time, the prevailing sentiment as to what constitutes a durable store of value can shift, and as such it is… pic.twitter.com/7XfBwAWFGi\n\n— Congressman Nick Begich (@RepNickBegich) May 21, 2026\n\n\nJoining Begich as the bill’s primary Democratic co-lead is Rep. Jared Golden (D-ME). The ARMA Act meanwhile counts 17 original co-sponsors, including Rep. Mike Collins (R-GA), among the first lawmakers to disclose an investment in a meme coin on Capitol Hill.\n\n“Administrations have auctioned [crypto] off or held it in reserve, according to the whims of the executive branch,” Golden said in a statement, noting that a stockpile created under the ARMA Act would enjoy the “weight of law.”\n\nAlthough a former administration official had said that the White House was eager to purchase as much Bitcoin as possible in a “budget-neutral way,” Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent later dented enthusiasm by ruling out any agency purchases.\n\nThe bill is aimed at providing the flexibility needed to broaden America’s portfolio of reserve assets that effectively serve as insurance policy, while also safeguarding crypto from “the whims of Congress or future administrations,” Begich said in an X post on Thursday.\n\nEstablished in May 2025 with a stroke of the president’s pen, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin was swiftly skewered by critics, including Rep. Maxine Waters (D-CA), the ranking member of the House Financial Services Committee, who warned of benefits for Trump insiders.\n\n“Typically, when the government holds strategic reserves, it is for an essential input that powers the U.S. economy and day-to-day life for American families,” she said. “Crypto, however, does not fall into these categories, because it has no inherent value.”\n\nAlthough the much-hyped stockpile’s creation represented a symbolic win for the digital assets industry, the government has yet to announce any allocations. Last year, Sen. Cynthia Lummis (R-WY) re-introduced the BITCOIN Act, a bill that directs the Treasury to purchase 1 million Bitcoin over five years in a way that seeks to avoid taxing citizens.\n\n*Decrypt* has reached out to Begich and Golden for comment.","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779437987864}}
{"type":"message","id":"6f24a02e","parentId":"d54090b2","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:47.865Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_alr1lh3gRuGPrUrZ03yKrygw","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4623 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptoslate_arma.md\n# US lawmakers push new Strategic Bitcoin Reserve act to secure $25 billion federal stash\n\nARMA would force the government to hold its Bitcoin for two decades to build a strategic financial buffer.\n\nA renewed push to solidify the United States’ foothold in the digital asset market emerged Thursday as lawmakers unveiled legislation to establish a formal strategic Bitcoin reserve and consolidate the federal government's multibillion-dollar cryptocurrency stockpile.\n\nSpearheaded by Representative Nick Begich, the American Reserve Modernization Act (ARMA) of 2026 mandates the creation of a secure Bitcoin reserve within the US Department of the Treasury, alongside a separate “Digital Asset Stockpile” for federally held, non-Bitcoin cryptocurrencies.\n\nThe legislation aims to provide a long-term structure to the government’s existing digital wealth, primarily amassed through law enforcement seizures. According to data from Bitcoin Treasuries, the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin, a stash valued at more than $25 billion.\n\nBegich said in a statement:\n\n“The American Reserve Modernization Act positions the United States to lead confidently in the digital age while protecting taxpayer interests, strengthening financial sovereignty, and reinforcing the principles of transparency and sound stewardship.”\n\n\nThe bill has 17 original House co-sponsors, including Buddy Carter, Ben Cline of Virginia, Barry Moore of Alabama, Burgess Owens of Utah, Mariannette Miller-Meeks of Iowa, Mike Carey and Michael Rulli of Ohio, Mike Collins of Georgia, Mike Lawler of New York, Riley Moore of West Virginia, and Tim Moore of North Carolina.\n\nApart from the strong legislative support, crypto industry leaders are already throwing their weight behind the measure.\n\nMatt Cole, CEO of Bitcoin treasury firm Strive, called the proposal “the single most important crypto legislation that can come out of D.C.” for the long-term health and security of the United States.\n\n## ARMA establishes a 20-year Bitcoin bet\n\nIf enacted, the bill would radically alter how Washington handles digital assets. Rather than periodically auctioning off seized crypto, the Treasury would be forced to consolidate custody and oversight across all federal agencies.\n\nCrucially, the legislation requires that any Bitcoin held in the strategic reserve be maintained for at least 20 years, effectively turning the US government into a long-term “hodler.”\n\nTo address persistent industry demands for transparency, the bill establishes stringent reporting measures. The Treasury would be required to publish quarterly public “Proof of Reserve” reports, submit to independent third-party audits, and face direct congressional oversight.\n\nThe legislation also directs a study into budget-neutral acquisition strategies. The goal is to evaluate lawful methods for the government to expand its digital reserves without relying on tax increases, deficit spending, or adding to the national debt.\n\nFurthermore, the bill offers an olive branch to crypto advocates by explicitly protecting self-custody rights, affirming that the federal government may not impair Americans' lawful right to own, transfer, or independently secure their digital assets.\n\n## Mounting pro-Bitcoin momentum in Washington\n\nBegich’s proposal builds on a broader, ongoing effort in Washington to pivot the US toward a pro-crypto stance.\n\nLast year, Sen. Cynthia Lummis proposed the BITCOIN Act, which would require the United States to acquire 1 million Bitcoin over five years, hold the assets for at least 20 years, and distribute storage across secure sites nationwide.\n\nThe bill also includes a financing mechanism tied to Federal Reserve gold certificates, a structure that would make the proposal a direct bet on converting part of the federal balance sheet into Bitcoin exposure.\n\nLummis legislation followed President Donald Trump's executive order to establish a strategic Bitcoin reserve.\n\nHowever, these initiatives have considerably stalled despite the Trump administration's pro-crypto stance.\n\nStill, the Begich bill aims to build on the recent advancement of the Clarity Act, which passed the Senate Banking Committee with bipartisan support. That bill is designed to establish clear rules of the road for the industry and encourage institutional investment.\n\nBitcoin is **-0.25%** over the past 24 hours and currently sits at rank #1 by market cap.\n\n## Where the broader market sits right now\n\nRight now, the total crypto market is valued at **$2.58T** with **$77.13B** in 24-hour volume. Bitcoin dominance sits at **60.01%**. Explore the market"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":2298,"aggregated":"4623 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptoslate_arma.md\n# US lawmakers push new Strategic Bitcoin Reserve act to secure $25 billion federal stash\n\nARMA would force the government to hold its Bitcoin for two decades to build a strategic financial buffer.\n\nA renewed push to solidify the United States’ foothold in the digital asset market emerged Thursday as lawmakers unveiled legislation to establish a formal strategic Bitcoin reserve and consolidate the federal government's multibillion-dollar cryptocurrency stockpile.\n\nSpearheaded by Representative Nick Begich, the American Reserve Modernization Act (ARMA) of 2026 mandates the creation of a secure Bitcoin reserve within the US Department of the Treasury, alongside a separate “Digital Asset Stockpile” for federally held, non-Bitcoin cryptocurrencies.\n\nThe legislation aims to provide a long-term structure to the government’s existing digital wealth, primarily amassed through law enforcement seizures. According to data from Bitcoin Treasuries, the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin, a stash valued at more than $25 billion.\n\nBegich said in a statement:\n\n“The American Reserve Modernization Act positions the United States to lead confidently in the digital age while protecting taxpayer interests, strengthening financial sovereignty, and reinforcing the principles of transparency and sound stewardship.”\n\n\nThe bill has 17 original House co-sponsors, including Buddy Carter, Ben Cline of Virginia, Barry Moore of Alabama, Burgess Owens of Utah, Mariannette Miller-Meeks of Iowa, Mike Carey and Michael Rulli of Ohio, Mike Collins of Georgia, Mike Lawler of New York, Riley Moore of West Virginia, and Tim Moore of North Carolina.\n\nApart from the strong legislative support, crypto industry leaders are already throwing their weight behind the measure.\n\nMatt Cole, CEO of Bitcoin treasury firm Strive, called the proposal “the single most important crypto legislation that can come out of D.C.” for the long-term health and security of the United States.\n\n## ARMA establishes a 20-year Bitcoin bet\n\nIf enacted, the bill would radically alter how Washington handles digital assets. Rather than periodically auctioning off seized crypto, the Treasury would be forced to consolidate custody and oversight across all federal agencies.\n\nCrucially, the legislation requires that any Bitcoin held in the strategic reserve be maintained for at least 20 years, effectively turning the US government into a long-term “hodler.”\n\nTo address persistent industry demands for transparency, the bill establishes stringent reporting measures. The Treasury would be required to publish quarterly public “Proof of Reserve” reports, submit to independent third-party audits, and face direct congressional oversight.\n\nThe legislation also directs a study into budget-neutral acquisition strategies. The goal is to evaluate lawful methods for the government to expand its digital reserves without relying on tax increases, deficit spending, or adding to the national debt.\n\nFurthermore, the bill offers an olive branch to crypto advocates by explicitly protecting self-custody rights, affirming that the federal government may not impair Americans' lawful right to own, transfer, or independently secure their digital assets.\n\n## Mounting pro-Bitcoin momentum in Washington\n\nBegich’s proposal builds on a broader, ongoing effort in Washington to pivot the US toward a pro-crypto stance.\n\nLast year, Sen. Cynthia Lummis proposed the BITCOIN Act, which would require the United States to acquire 1 million Bitcoin over five years, hold the assets for at least 20 years, and distribute storage across secure sites nationwide.\n\nThe bill also includes a financing mechanism tied to Federal Reserve gold certificates, a structure that would make the proposal a direct bet on converting part of the federal balance sheet into Bitcoin exposure.\n\nLummis legislation followed President Donald Trump's executive order to establish a strategic Bitcoin reserve.\n\nHowever, these initiatives have considerably stalled despite the Trump administration's pro-crypto stance.\n\nStill, the Begich bill aims to build on the recent advancement of the Clarity Act, which passed the Senate Banking Committee with bipartisan support. That bill is designed to establish clear rules of the road for the industry and encourage institutional investment.\n\nBitcoin is **-0.25%** over the past 24 hours and currently sits at rank #1 by market cap.\n\n## Where the broader market sits right now\n\nRight now, the total crypto market is valued at **$2.58T** with **$77.13B** in 24-hour volume. Bitcoin dominance sits at **60.01%**. Explore the market","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779437987864}}
{"type":"message","id":"502f1772","parentId":"6f24a02e","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:19:47.871Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7ewiJbnT4g8HhVyu8M5LMPLm","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2833 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptofox_arma.md\nUS Representative **Nick Begich** has introduced the **Reserve Modernization Act**, a legislative proposal designed to formalize the role of digital assets within the national treasury. Supported by **16 original co-sponsors**, the bill seeks to establish a permanent **strategic Bitcoin reserve**, integrating federally held digital assets into a long-term storage framework. This initiative marks a significant shift in federal fiscal policy, aiming to treat **BTC** as a cornerstone of the nation's financial security for the coming decades.\n\n## Legislative Framework and 20-Year Lock-In Period\n\nThe proposed act focuses on the long-term preservation of digital assets. Under the terms of the legislation, any Bitcoin held within the strategic reserve would be **locked for a minimum period of 20 years**. This provision is intended to prevent short-term liquidation and ensure that the asset serves as a generational store of value. *By mandating a two-decade holding period, the bill aims to insulate the reserve from market volatility and shifting political priorities.*\n\nThe bill also outlines strategies for the **integration of existing federally held digital assets**, which often originate from law enforcement seizures. Rather than auctioning these assets, the government would transition them into the sovereign reserve.\n\n## Budget Neutrality and Legal Protections\n\nA key component of the Reserve Modernization Act is its focus on **budget-neutral acquisition strategies**. Lawmakers are exploring methods to expand the Bitcoin holdings without increasing the national deficit or requiring new taxpayer funding. Furthermore, the bill includes specific legal safeguards to:\n\n- Protect the reserve from\n**future liquidation efforts**by subsequent administrations. - Establish clear jurisdictional oversight for the management of the\n**blockchain-based assets**. - Codify the reserve into\n**official law**, providing a more permanent foundation than previous executive orders.\n\n*While prior administrative actions had moved toward creating a reserve through executive authority, this legislative approach seeks to provide a statutory mandate that would be significantly harder to reverse.*\n\nIn conclusion, the introduction of the Reserve Modernization Act on **May 22, 2026**, represents a major step toward the institutionalization of cryptocurrency at the federal level. By proposing a mandatory holding period and budget-neutral growth, the bill aims to stabilize the national balance sheet through the inclusion of decentralized assets. The progress of this legislation will be closely monitored by global markets as an indicator of the United States' long-term stance on **digital finance** and sovereign asset management.\n\n## Frequently Asked *Questions*\n\nQuick answers to the most common questions about this topic."}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":2410,"aggregated":"2833 /tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptofox_arma.md\nUS Representative **Nick Begich** has introduced the **Reserve Modernization Act**, a legislative proposal designed to formalize the role of digital assets within the national treasury. Supported by **16 original co-sponsors**, the bill seeks to establish a permanent **strategic Bitcoin reserve**, integrating federally held digital assets into a long-term storage framework. This initiative marks a significant shift in federal fiscal policy, aiming to treat **BTC** as a cornerstone of the nation's financial security for the coming decades.\n\n## Legislative Framework and 20-Year Lock-In Period\n\nThe proposed act focuses on the long-term preservation of digital assets. Under the terms of the legislation, any Bitcoin held within the strategic reserve would be **locked for a minimum period of 20 years**. This provision is intended to prevent short-term liquidation and ensure that the asset serves as a generational store of value. *By mandating a two-decade holding period, the bill aims to insulate the reserve from market volatility and shifting political priorities.*\n\nThe bill also outlines strategies for the **integration of existing federally held digital assets**, which often originate from law enforcement seizures. Rather than auctioning these assets, the government would transition them into the sovereign reserve.\n\n## Budget Neutrality and Legal Protections\n\nA key component of the Reserve Modernization Act is its focus on **budget-neutral acquisition strategies**. Lawmakers are exploring methods to expand the Bitcoin holdings without increasing the national deficit or requiring new taxpayer funding. Furthermore, the bill includes specific legal safeguards to:\n\n- Protect the reserve from\n**future liquidation efforts**by subsequent administrations. - Establish clear jurisdictional oversight for the management of the\n**blockchain-based assets**. - Codify the reserve into\n**official law**, providing a more permanent foundation than previous executive orders.\n\n*While prior administrative actions had moved toward creating a reserve through executive authority, this legislative approach seeks to provide a statutory mandate that would be significantly harder to reverse.*\n\nIn conclusion, the introduction of the Reserve Modernization Act on **May 22, 2026**, represents a major step toward the institutionalization of cryptocurrency at the federal level. By proposing a mandatory holding period and budget-neutral growth, the bill aims to stabilize the national balance sheet through the inclusion of decentralized assets. The progress of this legislation will be closely monitored by global markets as an indicator of the United States' long-term stance on **digital finance** and sovereign asset management.\n\n## Frequently Asked *Questions*\n\nQuick answers to the most common questions about this topic.","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779437987864}}
{"type":"message","id":"0c7da869","parentId":"502f1772","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:20:13.546Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_Fkg309jaiZpCn67matJR1mz6","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, textwrap\nout='/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/raw.json'\nfiles={\n 'Decrypt':'/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/decrypt_arma.md',\n 'CryptoSlate':'/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptoslate_arma.md',\n 'CryptoFox':'/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-081534/research/articles/cryptofox_arma.md',\n}\ntexts={k:open(v).read().strip() for k,v in files.items()}\naggregated='\\n\\n'.join([f'SOURCE: {k}\\n{texts[k]}' for k in ['Decrypt','CryptoSlate','CryptoFox']])\nobj={\n  'status':'ok',\n  'story_id':'strategic-bitcoin-reserve-arma-2026-05-22',\n  'topic_theme':'US Strategic Bitcoin Reserve Legislation',\n  'primary_keyword':'Bitcoin reserve',\n  'primary_headline':'US lawmakers introduce ARMA bill to lock a federal Strategic Bitcoin Reserve into law',\n  'primary_asset':'Bitcoin',\n  'chart_coin':'bitcoin',\n  'sources_used':['Decrypt','CryptoSlate','CryptoFox'],\n  'source_urls':[\n    'https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law',\n    'https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/',\n    'https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve'\n  ],\n  'combined_key_facts':[\n    'Rep. Nick Begich introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act (ARMA) with Democratic co-lead Jared Golden, aiming to codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve in federal law instead of relying only on executive action.',\n    'The bill would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a Bitcoin reserve for at least 20 years, while also establishing a separate digital-asset stockpile for non-Bitcoin crypto held by the government.',\n    'ARMA would consolidate federally held digital assets across agencies, much of it obtained through forfeitures and penalties, and require public proof-of-reserve style transparency reporting.',\n    'CryptoSlate says the US currently holds 328,372 BTC valued at more than $25 billion, giving the proposal immediate scale if existing holdings are folded into the reserve.',\n    'The legislation calls for quarterly public reserve reports, independent third-party audits, and congressional oversight, while also studying budget-neutral ways to expand holdings without tax increases or deficit spending.',\n    'The proposal is designed to make federal Bitcoin custody harder for future administrations to unwind and, according to CryptoSlate, also explicitly protects Americans\\' lawful self-custody rights.'\n  ],\n  'aggregated_raw_content':aggregated,\n  'tweet_quotes':[]\n}\nos.makedirs(os.path.dirname(out), exist_ok=True)\nwith open(out,'w') as f:\n    json.dump(obj,f,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)\nprint(out)\nprint('words', len(aggregated.split()))\nPY","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":10000,"timeout":60}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":95642,"output":1535,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":97177,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779437987872,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiFOftPIdEpVcKuAftmIkpzrUBhft"}}
